  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "Given that the tangent line approximation to the function $f(x)$ at $x=-1$ underestimates the values of the function in the neighborhood of $x=-1,$ which of the following must be true?",
  "worked_solution": "Since the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=-1$ underestimates the values of the function, we have\n\n$$\nL(x)<f(x),\n$$\n\nthat is, the line $L(x)$ must be below the graph of $f(x)$ near the point  $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the function must be concave up in the neighborhood of $x=-1.$",
